/* Shortcut Press — Interface Design in Figma guide content (generated by tools/site/build.mjs). */
window.SP_GUIDE = {"id":"figma-interface-design","slug":"figma-interface-design","title":"Interface Design in Figma","software":"Figma","apps":["Figma"],"intro":"Design app screens in Figma: frames, auto layout, components, styles, and prototyping a simple app screen flow. The lessons build towards a two-screen sign-in flow using the practice assets.","platformNote":"Shortcuts are shown for Windows and ChromeOS. On a Mac, use Cmd in place of Ctrl and Option in place of Alt.","files":[{"name":"icon-set.svg","title":"Icon set","desc":"Eight simple line icons on a 24 px grid, ready to drag into a Figma file.","size":"2 KB"},{"name":"ui-pieces.svg","title":"Interface pieces","desc":"A button, a text field, a card and a toggle, drawn as plain vector shapes to rebuild with auto layout.","size":"1 KB"},{"name":"sign-in-wireframe.svg","title":"Sign-in wireframe","desc":"A gray-box sketch of the sign-in and home screens used in the prototyping lesson.","size":"1 KB"}],"shortcuts":[{"keys":"V","action":"Move tool","app":"Figma"},{"keys":"F","action":"Frame tool","app":"Figma"},{"keys":"R","action":"Rectangle tool","app":"Figma"},{"keys":"O","action":"Ellipse tool","app":"Figma"},{"keys":"T","action":"Text tool","app":"Figma"},{"keys":"P","action":"Pen tool","app":"Figma"},{"keys":"Shift+A","action":"Add auto layout","app":"Figma"},{"keys":"Ctrl+Alt+K","action":"Create a component","app":"Figma"},{"keys":"Ctrl+Alt+B","action":"Detach an instance","app":"Figma"},{"keys":"Ctrl+G","action":"Group the selection","app":"Figma"},{"keys":"Ctrl+Shift+G","action":"Ungroup","app":"Figma"},{"keys":"Ctrl+Alt+G","action":"Wrap the selection in a frame","app":"Figma"},{"keys":"Ctrl+D","action":"Duplicate","app":"Figma"},{"keys":"Shift+1","action":"Zoom to fit everything","app":"Figma"},{"keys":"Shift+2","action":"Zoom to the selection","app":"Figma"},{"keys":"Ctrl+Alt+Enter","action":"Present the prototype","app":"Figma"},{"keys":"Ctrl+Z","action":"Undo","app":"Figma"}],"lessons":[{"id":"frames","title":"Frames","summary":"Set up frames for different screen sizes, nest frames, and use layout guides to line things up.","diagram":{"type":"frames","variant":"nest","title":"Frames nested inside a screen frame"},"steps":[{"title":"Frames are screens and containers","body":"<p>A <strong>frame</strong> is Figma's container: a whole screen, a card, or a button. Press <kbd>F</kbd> for the Frame tool; the right sidebar then lists preset sizes (Phone, Tablet, Desktop and more). Click one, or drag on the canvas to draw any size.</p>"},{"title":"Nesting and clipping","body":"<p>Draw a frame inside another and it becomes a child: it moves with its parent and appears in the Layers panel nested under it. Select shapes and press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>Alt</kbd> <kbd>G</kbd> to wrap them in a new frame. A frame's <em>Clip content</em> option hides anything that spills over its edges.</p>"},{"title":"Layout guides","body":"<p>Select a frame and add a layout guide in the Design panel (shown as layout grids in older versions of Figma). Choose <em>Columns</em> (for example 4 columns with 16 px margins and gutters on a phone), <em>Rows</em>, or a square <em>Grid</em>. Objects snap to them as you move.</p>"},{"title":"Bring in the practice files","body":"<p>Drag an SVG file from your computer onto the canvas to import it as editable vector layers. Press <kbd>Shift</kbd> <kbd>1</kbd> to zoom so everything fits, and <kbd>Shift</kbd> <kbd>2</kbd> to zoom to the selection.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which shortcut wraps selected layers in a new frame?","options":["Ctrl+G","Ctrl+Alt+G","Shift+A","Ctrl+D"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ctrl+G makes a group; Ctrl+Alt+G makes a frame."},{"q":"What does Clip content do?","options":["Deletes hidden layers","Hides anything outside the frame's edges","Locks the frame","Exports the frame"],"answer":1,"explain":"Clipping hides child content that extends beyond the frame."}],"exercise":{"title":"Set up two phone screens","task":"<p>In a new Figma design file, make two phone-sized frames named “Sign in” and “Home”, each with a 4-column layout guide, and import <strong>sign-in-wireframe.svg</strong> beside them for reference.</p>","files":["sign-in-wireframe.svg"],"solution":["Press <kbd>F</kbd>, choose a phone preset in the right sidebar, and click. Double-click the frame's name in the Layers panel and rename it “Sign in”.","With it selected, press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>D</kbd> to duplicate, move the copy to the right, and rename it “Home”.","Select both frames and add a layout guide in the Design panel: Columns, Count 4, Margin 16, Gutter 16.","Drag sign-in-wireframe.svg onto the canvas, then press <kbd>Shift</kbd> <kbd>1</kbd>."]}},{"id":"auto-layout","title":"Auto layout","summary":"Build buttons, lists and cards that resize with their content, and control spacing, padding and alignment.","diagram":{"type":"frames","variant":"autolayout","title":"Auto layout sets the gap and padding once"},"steps":[{"title":"Add auto layout","body":"<p>Select a frame, or several layers, and press <kbd>Shift</kbd> <kbd>A</kbd>. Figma arranges the contents in a row or column and the frame now grows and shrinks with them. Type more text into a button and the button widens by itself.</p>"},{"title":"Direction, gap and padding","body":"<p>In the auto layout section of the Design panel, choose vertical or horizontal direction, set the <em>gap</em> between items, and set <em>padding</em> around the edges, either equal on all sides or separately. Alignment controls place items at the start, center or end. Setting the gap to <em>Auto</em> spreads items evenly across the space.</p>"},{"title":"Resizing: hug, fixed and fill","body":"<p>Each layer in auto layout has width and height settings:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Hug contents:</strong> as big as what's inside.</li>\n<li><strong>Fixed:</strong> an exact size.</li>\n<li><strong>Fill container:</strong> stretches to fill the parent.</li></ul>\n<p>A full-width button inside a screen: set the screen's content column to auto layout and the button's width to Fill.</p>"},{"title":"Nest auto layouts","body":"<p>Real screens are auto layouts inside auto layouts: a card is a vertical stack of an image, a text block and a horizontal row of buttons. Build from the inside out: text and icon first, then the button around them, then the row of buttons, then the card.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which shortcut adds auto layout?","options":["Shift+A","Ctrl+A","Alt+A","A"],"answer":0,"explain":"Shift+A adds auto layout to the selection."},{"q":"You want a button to stretch across its parent. Which width setting?","options":["Hug contents","Fixed","Fill container","Auto gap"],"answer":2,"explain":"Fill container makes a child expand to the available space."}],"exercise":{"title":"Rebuild the button and text field","task":"<p>Import <strong>ui-pieces.svg</strong>. Rebuild the button with auto layout so it grows with its label, using 12 px vertical and 20 px horizontal padding, and put a label above the text field with an 8 px gap.</p>","files":["ui-pieces.svg"],"solution":["Press <kbd>T</kbd> and type “Sign in”. Select the text and press <kbd>Shift</kbd> <kbd>A</kbd>; Figma wraps it in an auto layout frame.","Set the padding to 12 top and bottom and 20 left and right, give the frame a fill color, and set the corner radius to match the original.","Type a longer label; the button widens.","Select a text layer “Email” and the field shape, press <kbd>Shift</kbd> <kbd>A</kbd>, choose vertical direction and set the gap to 8."]}},{"id":"components","title":"Components","summary":"Turn repeated elements into components, use instances, change an instance without breaking the link, and organize variants.","diagram":{"type":"components"},"steps":[{"title":"Create a component","body":"<p>Select a finished element, such as the button, and press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>Alt</kbd> <kbd>K</kbd> (or choose <em>Create component</em> in the toolbar). It becomes a <strong>main component</strong>, marked with a purple outline and label.</p>"},{"title":"Use instances","body":"<p>Copies of a component are <strong>instances</strong>. Drag one from the <strong>Assets</strong> tab in the left sidebar, or copy and paste the main component. Change the main component's color or radius and every instance updates.</p>"},{"title":"Overrides","body":"<p>You can change an instance's text, colors and nested instances without breaking the link. These are overrides, and they're kept when the main component changes. <strong>Reset all changes</strong> in the instance's right-click menu removes them; <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>Alt</kbd> <kbd>B</kbd> detaches an instance into a plain frame, ending the link.</p>"},{"title":"Variants","body":"<p>Related versions of a component, such as a primary and secondary button, or default and disabled, can be combined into one set. Select the components and choose <strong>Combine as variants</strong> in the right sidebar, then name the properties (for example <em>Style = Primary</em>). On an instance, switch variant from the Design panel.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"You edit the main component's color. What happens to its instances?","options":["Nothing","They all update","Only the newest updates","They detach"],"answer":1,"explain":"Instances follow their main component, except for properties you've overridden."},{"q":"What does Ctrl+Alt+B do on an instance?","options":["Makes it bold","Detaches it from the component","Creates a variant","Groups it"],"answer":1,"explain":"Detaching turns the instance into an ordinary frame with no link."}],"exercise":{"title":"A button component with two variants","task":"<p>Make your auto layout button a component, create a secondary version with an outline instead of a fill, combine them as variants, and place two instances on the Sign in screen with different labels.</p>","files":["ui-pieces.svg"],"solution":["Select the button and press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>Alt</kbd> <kbd>K</kbd>.","Duplicate the main component (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>D</kbd>), remove its fill and add a stroke.","Select both main components and choose Combine as variants. Rename the property to Style, with values Primary and Secondary.","From the Assets tab, drag two instances onto the Sign in frame. Change one label to “Create account” and switch it to the Secondary variant."]}},{"id":"styles","title":"Styles","summary":"Save colors, text settings and effects as styles so a whole design can be updated from one place.","diagram":{"type":"compare","title":"Styles and the layers that use them","left":{"title":"Styles","items":["Color: Brand","Color: Surface","Text: Heading","Text: Body","Effect: Card shadow"]},"right":{"title":"Used by","items":["Buttons, links","Screens, cards","Screen titles","All paragraphs","Cards, menus"]},"accentRight":true},"steps":[{"title":"Color styles","body":"<p>Select a layer with the fill you want to reuse. In the Fill section of the Design panel, open the styles button and choose <strong>+</strong> (Create style). Name it, for example “Brand”. Apply it to other layers from the same button.</p>"},{"title":"Text and effect styles","body":"<p>Select a text layer, open the styles button in the Text section, and create a text style that saves the font, size, weight and line height. Effects such as drop shadows can be saved the same way. Names with a slash, such as “Text/Heading”, group styles into folders.</p>"},{"title":"Update everything at once","body":"<p>Deselect everything to see the file's local styles in the right sidebar. Edit a style there and every layer using it changes. Newer versions of Figma also offer <em>variables</em> for colors and numbers; styles remain fully supported and are the simplest place to start.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What happens when you edit a color style?","options":["Only new layers use the change","Every layer using that style updates","The style is duplicated","The file is exported"],"answer":1,"explain":"Styles are shared, so editing one changes everything that uses it."},{"q":"How do you group styles into folders?","options":["Use a slash in the name, such as Text/Heading","Drag them into a frame","Use Ctrl+G","Create a component"],"answer":0,"explain":"A slash in a style name creates a folder in the styles list."}],"exercise":{"title":"Style the sign-in screen","task":"<p>Create a Brand color style, a Heading and a Body text style, and apply them to the Sign in screen. Then change the Brand color and check both buttons update.</p>","files":["sign-in-wireframe.svg"],"solution":["Select the primary button's fill and create a color style named Brand.","Select the screen title, set the font and size you want, and create a text style named Text/Heading. Do the same for a paragraph as Text/Body.","Apply the styles to the remaining text layers.","Deselect everything, edit Brand in the right sidebar's local styles, and watch the button (and the secondary variant's outline, if it uses Brand) update."]}},{"id":"prototyping","title":"Prototyping a screen flow","summary":"Link screens together, choose interactions and animations, and preview a simple app flow from start to finish.","diagram":{"type":"prototype"},"steps":[{"title":"Switch to Prototype mode","body":"<p>Open the <strong>Prototype</strong> tab at the top of the right sidebar. Select a layer, such as the Sign in button; a small circle appears on its edge. Drag it onto the destination frame to create a connection.</p>"},{"title":"Interactions and animations","body":"<p>Each connection has an <strong>interaction</strong>: a trigger (<em>On click</em>, <em>On drag</em>, <em>While hovering</em>…) and an action (<em>Navigate to</em>, <em>Back</em>, <em>Open overlay</em>…). Choose an <strong>animation</strong>: Instant, Dissolve, a slide, or Smart animate, which moves matching layers smoothly between screens.</p>"},{"title":"Flow starting points","body":"<p>The first frame you connect becomes a flow starting point, shown in the Prototype tab. Rename the flow (for example “Sign in flow”) so it's easy to find and share. A file can have several flows.</p>"},{"title":"Present and test","body":"<p>Select the Present button at the top right (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>Alt</kbd> <kbd>Enter</kbd>) to open the prototype and click through it. In prototype settings you can choose a device frame. Add a <em>Back</em> action on the Home screen so testers can return.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which animation moves matching layers smoothly between two screens?","options":["Instant","Dissolve","Smart animate","Move in"],"answer":2,"explain":"Smart animate matches layers by name and animates the differences."},{"q":"Where do you create connections between screens?","options":["The Design tab","The Prototype tab","The Assets tab","File > Export"],"answer":1,"explain":"Connections are made in Prototype mode in the right sidebar."}],"exercise":{"title":"Prototype the sign-in flow","task":"<p>Connect the Sign in button to the Home screen with On click → Navigate to and a Dissolve, add a way back, and present the prototype.</p>","files":["sign-in-wireframe.svg"],"solution":["Open the Prototype tab and select the Sign in button on the Sign in frame.","Drag its connection circle onto the Home frame. Set On click → Navigate to → Home, animation Dissolve, about 300 ms.","On Home, select a layer to act as a back link (or the whole frame), connect it with On click → Back.","Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>Alt</kbd> <kbd>Enter</kbd> and click through: Sign in → Home → back to Sign in."]}}],"versionNote":"Software makers update their programs, so menus and button locations can change between versions. This guide is revised when an update changes the steps, and the revised version appears the next time you open it."};
